/*
midi.lcb - xTalk Builder (LCB) binding library for realtime MIDI I/O, via the
           midi_shim C layer over RtMidi. The midixt member of the xTalk suite
           (born as the MIDI third of ShowControl for OpenXTalk).

It targets OpenXTalk / the xTalk language family and is compatible with
LiveCode 9.6.3+. Once this extension is loaded, every public handler below is
callable directly from xTalk script:

    local tOut
    put midiOpenOutput(0) into tOut
    if tOut is 0 then answer midiLastError()
    midiNoteOn tOut, 1, 60, 100

Output is request/response. Input uses RtMidi's internal FIFO plus POLLING: the
script (or the shipped dispatcher, examples/midi-helpers) calls midiPoll on a
timer and drains the queue. Nothing here calls back into script from a foreign
thread (suite rule 1). Because RtMidi buffers and delta-time-stamps every
message, message integrity and timing survive jittery poll cadence; only added
latency scales with the interval.

Conventions:
  * Handles are integers (0 = invalid); a stale handle is a harmless no-op.
  * Channels in the public API are 1-based (1..16). Raw MIDI bytes are 0-based
    on the wire; the convenience senders convert.
  * MIDI byte buffers are LiveCode `Data`. A Data does NOT auto-bridge to a
    foreign `Pointer` (it marshals as an MCDataRef), so -- exactly as osc.lcb and
    the other suite bindings do -- sends pass MCDataGetBytePtr(theData), and the
    drain fills a raw engine block (MCMemoryAllocate) whose written bytes are
    copied back with MCDataCreateWithBytes.
  * No binding RETURNS a C string: the engine free()s a returned ZString, which
    crashes on the shim's static name/error buffers. Names and the last error
    come back through the caller-buffer entry points instead (see shimText).
  * Nothing here throws for bad INPUT: a refusal returns 0 / does nothing and
    midiLastError() says why. A throw means the native library is missing or is
    not the ABI this binding was built for.

The foreign handlers bind to the shared library "midixt" (built by
CMakeLists.txt, bundled inside the extension under
code/<arch>-<platform>/midixt.{so,dll,dylib}). The exported C ABI symbols keep
the stable "midi_" prefix.
*/

/**
Sends and receives MIDI: the messages keyboards, controllers, synthesizers,
lighting desks and music software use to talk to each other.

Open a port with <midiOpenOutput> or <midiOpenInput> (their numbers come from
<midiOutputPorts> and <midiInputPorts>), or make one of your own that other
programs can connect to with <midiOpenVirtualOutput> and
<midiOpenVirtualInput> (macOS and Linux). Send with <midiNoteOn>,
<midiControlChange> and the other senders, or any bytes at all with
<midiSend>. Incoming MIDI waits in a queue until you collect it with
<midiPoll>, which a timer calls; the midi-helpers script
(examples/midi-helpers.livecodescript) runs that timer for you and sends an
`scNoteOn`, `scMidiCC` or other message for each event. Close every port with
<midiClose>.

Nothing here throws for a mistake in what you pass. A port that will not open
returns 0, and <midiLastError> says why.

Built on RtMidi by Gary P. Scavone.

Tags: MIDI, Music, Show control, Library
*/
library org.openxtalk.library.midi

use com.livecode.foreign

metadata title is "MIDI for OpenXTalk"
metadata author is "Seth Morrow Software"
metadata version is "0.1.0"

-- =====================================================================
-- Foreign bindings to the shim (private)
-- =====================================================================
private foreign handler _abi_version() returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_abi_version!cdecl"
private foreign handler _in_count() returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_in_count!cdecl"
private foreign handler _out_count() returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_out_count!cdecl"
private foreign handler _in_name(in pIndex as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_in_name!cdecl"
private foreign handler _out_name(in pIndex as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_out_name!cdecl"
private foreign handler _in_open(in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_in_open!cdecl"
private foreign handler _out_open(in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_out_open!cdecl"
private foreign handler _in_open_virtual(in pName as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_in_open_virtual!cdecl"
private foreign handler _out_open_virtual(in pName as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_out_open_virtual!cdecl"
private foreign handler _port_close(in pH as CInt) returns nothing binds to "c:midixt>midi_close!cdecl"
private foreign handler _in_ignore(in pH as CInt, in pSysex as CInt, in pTime as CInt, in pSense as CInt) returns nothing binds to "c:midixt>midi_in_ignore!cdecl"
-- midi_in_drain_bytes (ABI 2) returns the BYTES written, which is what we need to
-- copy exactly the drained records back out of the raw block.
private foreign handler _in_drain_bytes(in pH as CInt, in pOut as Pointer, in pCap as CInt, in pMax as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_in_drain_bytes!cdecl"
private foreign handler _out_send(in pH as CInt, in pData as optional Pointer, in pLen as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_out_send!cdecl"
private foreign handler _last_error(in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:midixt>midi_last_error!cdecl"

-- dlerror() returns libc-owned text: take it as a raw Pointer and COPY it with
-- MCStringCreateWithCString, never as a ZString the engine would free. (The
-- names carry an inner "_": lc-compile -Werror, the IDE's Extension Builder
-- flag, refuses an all-lowercase single-word name such as _dlopen.)
private foreign handler _dl_open(in pPath as ZStringNative, in pFlags as CInt) returns optional Pointer binds to "c:>dlopen!cdecl"
private foreign handler _dl_error() returns optional Pointer binds to "c:>dlerror!cdecl"

-- Engine <builtin> helpers (libfoundation/foundation.h). A `<builtin>` handler
-- resolves by its NAME matching the engine symbol, so these carry NO leading `_`.
private foreign handler MCDataGetBytePtr(in pData as Data) returns Pointer binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCMemoryAllocate(in pSize as UIntSize, out rBlock as Pointer) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCMemoryDeallocate(in pBlock as Pointer) returns nothing binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCDataCreateWithBytes(in pBytes as Pointer, in pCount as CUInt, out rData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCStringDecode(in pData as Data, in pEncoding as CInt, in pIsExternalRep as CBool, out rString as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCStringCreateWithCString(in pCString as Pointer, out rString as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"

-- =====================================================================
-- Raw buffers (reused -- never rebuilt in the poll hot path).
-- =====================================================================
-- The drain block: allocated ONCE (kDrainCap bytes) and reused every poll. The
-- flag guards the first allocation, so an unset Pointer is never passed.
variable sDrainPtr as Pointer
variable sDrainReady as Boolean
-- A small block for names / the last error, grown on demand. Its capacity (0
-- until the first allocation) doubles as the "is it allocated?" flag.
variable sTextPtr as Pointer
variable sTextCap as Integer

-- A refusal decided HERE, before anything reaches the shim (a NUL character in
-- a port name, which the engine cannot pass as a C string and would THROW
-- into script for). Each fallible public handler clears it on entry, as the
-- shim clears its own, and midiLastError() reports it ahead of the shim's.
variable sBindingError as String

-- The reason the last midiLoadNativeLib failed, read from dlerror() at the
-- moment of the failure and kept. Read there, not later: glibc frees the text
-- dlerror() returned at the NEXT dl* call in the process, and the engine makes
-- such calls itself, binding each foreign handler on its first call through
-- dlsym (engine note 6.19). ShowControl's midiLoadNativeLibError read dlerror()
-- when it was called: its first answer after a failure was empty, and its
-- second the freed text (OBSERVED under lc-run, 2026-11-21).
variable sLoadError as String

-- Must be >= the C shim's largest single drain record: a 2-byte length prefix, a
-- 65535-byte maximum SysEx payload, and a 4-byte delta = 65541 bytes. A smaller
-- cap lets an inbound message bigger than the buffer get stashed but never
-- re-emitted, permanently wedging the input port. (Allocated just once.)
private constant kDrainCap is 65541

-- LiveCode Builder has no integer-division operator -- 'div' is LiveCode Script
-- only and does not parse in LCB. Compute floor division for non-negative
-- integers: a - (a mod b) is an exact multiple of b, so dividing gives the exact
-- quotient. (Defined before first use.)
private handler intDiv(in pA as Integer, in pB as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tQ as Integer
   put (pA - (pA mod pB)) / pB into tQ
   return tQ
end handler

private handler ensureDrainBuf()
   variable tOk as Boolean
   if sDrainReady is true then
      return
   end if
   put false into tOk
   unsafe
      put MCMemoryAllocate(kDrainCap, sDrainPtr) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      throw "midi: failed to allocate the drain buffer"
   end if
   put true into sDrainReady
end handler

private handler ensureTextBuf(in pNeed as Integer)
   variable tOk as Boolean
   if sTextCap >= pNeed then
      return
   end if
   if sTextCap > 0 then
      unsafe
         MCMemoryDeallocate(sTextPtr)
      end unsafe
      put 0 into sTextCap
   end if
   put false into tOk
   unsafe
      put MCMemoryAllocate(pNeed, sTextPtr) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      throw "midi: failed to allocate a text buffer"
   end if
   put pNeed into sTextCap
end handler

-- Which caller-buffer string getter shimText calls (literal, declared before use).
private constant kTxtInName is 1
private constant kTxtOutName is 2
private constant kTxtError is 3

-- Fetch one string through the shim's caller-buffer contract: bytes written
-- (>= 0), or -needed when too small (grow once, retry). Decoded as UTF-8.
private handler shimText(in pWhich as Integer, in pIndex as Integer) returns String
   variable tRc as Integer
   variable tTry as Integer
   variable tNeed as Integer
   variable tData as Data
   variable tStr as String
   variable tOk as Boolean
   put 256 into tNeed
   put the empty string into tStr
   repeat with tTry from 1 up to 2
      ensureTextBuf(tNeed)
      unsafe
         if pWhich is kTxtInName then
            put _in_name(pIndex, sTextPtr, sTextCap) into tRc
         else if pWhich is kTxtOutName then
            put _out_name(pIndex, sTextPtr, sTextCap) into tRc
         else
            put _last_error(sTextPtr, sTextCap) into tRc
         end if
      end unsafe
      if tRc is 0 then
         return tStr
      end if
      if tRc > 0 then
         unsafe
            put MCDataCreateWithBytes(sTextPtr, tRc, tData) into tOk
         end unsafe
         if not tOk then
            return the empty string
         end if
         unsafe
            put MCStringDecode(tData, 4, false, tStr) into tOk
         end unsafe
         if not tOk then
            return the empty string
         end if
         return tStr
      end if
      if -tRc <= sTextCap then
         return the empty string
      end if
      put -tRc into tNeed
   end repeat
   return the empty string
end handler

-- Does this text hold a NUL? A NUL cannot cross as a ZString: the engine
-- THROWS "cannot export char U+0000 in nul-terminated string buffer" into
-- script at the call, where every other bad value is a quiet 0.
private handler hasNul(in pText as String) returns Boolean
   return pText contains (the char with code 0)
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- The library
-- =====================================================================
/**
Summary: Returns the ABI number of the native library that is loaded.

Returns: The ABI number; this binding needs 2.

Example:
put midiVersion() into field "status"

Description: Every call that lists or opens ports checks this number first
and throws when it is not 2, which means the native library and this binding
come from different releases.
*/
public handler midiVersion() returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _abi_version() into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

private handler checkABI()
   variable tV as Integer
   unsafe
      put _abi_version() into tV
   end unsafe
   if tV is not 2 then
      throw "midi: incompatible native library ABI (binding needs ABI 2) - rebuild/repackage the midixt library"
   end if
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Listing the ports
-- =====================================================================
private handler portNames(in pWhich as Integer) returns List
   variable tCount as Integer
   variable tList as List
   variable tIndex as Integer
   checkABI()
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   unsafe
      if pWhich is kTxtInName then
         put _in_count() into tCount
      else
         put _out_count() into tCount
      end if
   end unsafe
   put [] into tList
   if tCount > 0 then
      repeat with tIndex from 0 up to tCount - 1
         push shimText(pWhich, tIndex) onto tList
      end repeat
   end if
   return tList
end handler

/**
Summary: Lists the MIDI inputs this computer has.

Returns: A list of port names. The first is port 0, the second port 1, and
so on: pass that number to <midiOpenInput>.

Example:
put midiInputPorts() into tPorts
repeat with tIndex = 1 to the number of elements of tPorts
   put tIndex - 1 & ":" && tPorts[tIndex] & return after field "Inputs"
end repeat

Description: An empty list means no inputs, or no MIDI service at all: then
<midiLastError> says so.
*/
public handler midiInputPorts() returns List
   return portNames(kTxtInName)
end handler

/**
Summary: Lists the MIDI outputs this computer has.

Returns: A list of port names. The first is port 0, the second port 1, and
so on: pass that number to <midiOpenOutput>.

Example:
put midiOutputPorts() into tPorts

Description: An empty list means no outputs, or no MIDI service at all: then
<midiLastError> says so.
*/
public handler midiOutputPorts() returns List
   return portNames(kTxtOutName)
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Opening and closing ports
-- =====================================================================
/**
Summary: Opens a MIDI input so that its messages can be read.

pIndex: The port's number: 0 for the first name <midiInputPorts> lists, 1
for the second, and so on.

Returns: The port's handle, or 0 when it would not open (<midiLastError> says
why).

Example:
put midiOpenInput(0) into tIn
if tIn is 0 then answer "No MIDI in:" && midiLastError()

Description: Read what arrives with <midiPoll>, and close the port with
<midiClose> when you are done with it.
*/
public handler midiOpenInput(in pIndex as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tH as Integer
   checkABI()
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   unsafe
      put _in_open(pIndex) into tH
   end unsafe
   return tH
end handler

/**
Summary: Opens a MIDI output so that messages can be sent to it.

pIndex: The port's number: 0 for the first name <midiOutputPorts> lists, 1
for the second, and so on.

Returns: The port's handle, or 0 when it would not open (<midiLastError> says
why).

Example:
put midiOpenOutput(0) into tOut
if tOut is 0 then answer midiLastError()

Description: Close the port with <midiClose> when you are done with it.
*/
public handler midiOpenOutput(in pIndex as Integer) returns Integer
   variable tH as Integer
   checkABI()
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   unsafe
      put _out_open(pIndex) into tH
   end unsafe
   return tH
end handler

private handler openVirtual(in pIsInput as Boolean, in pName as String) returns Integer
   variable tH as Integer
   checkABI()
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   if hasNul(pName) then
      if pIsInput then
         put "midiOpenVirtualInput: a port name cannot contain a NUL character" into sBindingError
      else
         put "midiOpenVirtualOutput: a port name cannot contain a NUL character" into sBindingError
      end if
      return 0
   end if
   unsafe
      if pIsInput then
         put _in_open_virtual(pName) into tH
      else
         put _out_open_virtual(pName) into tH
      end if
   end unsafe
   return tH
end handler

/**
Summary: Makes a new MIDI input that other programs can send to (macOS and
Linux).

pName: The name other programs list it under. Empty gives "ShowControl In".

Returns: The port's handle, or 0 when it could not be made (<midiLastError>
says why).

Example:
put midiOpenVirtualInput("My Show In") into tIn

Description: Windows has no virtual ports: there this returns 0 and the error
names the fix, a loopback driver such as loopMIDI, whose ports
<midiOpenInput> opens like any other.
*/
public handler midiOpenVirtualInput(in pName as String) returns Integer
   return openVirtual(true, pName)
end handler

/**
Summary: Makes a new MIDI output that other programs can receive from (macOS
and Linux).

pName: The name other programs list it under. Empty gives "ShowControl Out".

Returns: The port's handle, or 0 when it could not be made (<midiLastError>
says why).

Example:
put midiOpenVirtualOutput("My Show Out") into tOut

Description: Windows has no virtual ports: there this returns 0 and the error
names the fix, a loopback driver such as loopMIDI, whose ports
<midiOpenOutput> opens like any other.
*/
public handler midiOpenVirtualOutput(in pName as String) returns Integer
   return openVirtual(false, pName)
end handler

/**
Summary: Closes a MIDI port.

pHandle: The handle an open call returned.

Example:
midiClose tOut

Description: After this the handle is dead: every call with it does nothing.
Closing 0, or a handle that is already closed, does nothing.
*/
public handler midiClose(in pHandle as Integer)
   unsafe
      _port_close(pHandle)
   end unsafe
end handler

private handler boolFlag(in pB as Boolean) returns Integer
   if pB then
      return 1
   end if
   return 0
end handler

/**
Summary: Chooses which kinds of message an input skips.

pHandle: The handle <midiOpenInput> or <midiOpenVirtualInput> returned.

pSysex: true to skip SysEx messages.

pTime: true to skip MIDI timing clock and time code.

pSense: true to skip active sensing.

Example:
midiIgnoreTypes tIn, false, true, true -- keep SysEx, skip clock and sensing

Description: A new input skips all three, as RtMidi does, so call this with
false for any kind you want to receive.
*/
public handler midiIgnoreTypes(in pHandle as Integer, in pSysex as Boolean, in pTime as Boolean, in pSense as Boolean)
   unsafe
      _in_ignore(pHandle, boolFlag(pSysex), boolFlag(pTime), boolFlag(pSense))
   end unsafe
end handler

-- Clamp a value into a single MIDI data byte (0..127). Installations feed junk
-- from sliders / fields / incoming data; a data byte with the high bit set is
-- illegal MIDI and desyncs any receiver, and numToByte of an out-of-range value
-- has engine-dependent behaviour. Clamp so the convenience API is junk-proof.
private handler clamp7(in pV as Integer) returns Integer
   if pV < 0 then
      return 0
   end if
   if pV > 127 then
      return 127
   end if
   return pV
end handler

-- Map a 1..16 channel to its 0..15 status-byte nibble, normalizing out-of-range.
-- LCB mod follows the dividend's sign, so a bare (pChannel - 1) mod 16 goes
-- negative for pChannel <= 0 and would corrupt the status byte.
private handler chanNib(in pChannel as Integer) returns Integer
   return (((pChannel - 1) mod 16) + 16) mod 16
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Sending
-- =====================================================================
/**
Summary: Sends any MIDI bytes to an output.

pHandle: The handle <midiOpenOutput> or <midiOpenVirtualOutput> returned.

pBytes: The message, as binary data: one complete MIDI message, or a whole
SysEx message (F0 ... F7).

Example:
midiSend tOut, numToByte(240) & numToByte(126) & numToByte(127) & numToByte(6) & numToByte(1) & numToByte(247)

Description: When nothing is sent -- a closed handle, empty data, or a send
the MIDI service refused -- <midiLastError> says why, and is empty after a
send that went out. On macOS and Windows a message that is not SysEx must be
one message of 1 to 3 bytes; Linux also takes several messages in one call.
*/
public handler midiSend(in pHandle as Integer, in pBytes as Data)
   variable tPtr as Pointer
   variable tLen as Integer
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   put the number of bytes in pBytes into tLen
   unsafe
      if tLen is 0 then
         _out_send(pHandle, nothing, 0)     -- the shim says why nothing went
      else
         put MCDataGetBytePtr(pBytes) into tPtr
         _out_send(pHandle, tPtr, tLen)
      end if
   end unsafe
end handler

-- Pack up to three bytes into Data and send.
private handler send3(in pHandle as Integer, in pStatus as Integer, in pD1 as Integer, in pD2 as Integer, in pLen as Integer)
   variable tMsg as Data
   put the byte with code (pStatus) into tMsg
   if pLen >= 2 then
      put the byte with code (clamp7(pD1)) after tMsg
   end if
   if pLen >= 3 then
      put the byte with code (clamp7(pD2)) after tMsg
   end if
   midiSend(pHandle, tMsg)
end handler

/**
Summary: Sends a Note On.

pHandle: The handle of an open output.

pChannel: The channel, 1 to 16.

pNote: The note number, 0 to 127 (60 is middle C).

pVelocity: How hard, 1 to 127; 0 is read as a Note Off by most receivers.

Example:
midiNoteOn tOut, 1, 60, 100

Description: A note or velocity outside 0 to 127 is clamped into it, and a
channel outside 1 to 16 wraps (17 is channel 1).
*/
public handler midiNoteOn(in pHandle as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pNote as Integer, in pVelocity as Integer)
   send3(pHandle, 144 + chanNib(pChannel), pNote, pVelocity, 3)
end handler

/**
Summary: Sends a Note Off.

pHandle: The handle of an open output.

pChannel: The channel, 1 to 16.

pNote: The note number, 0 to 127.

pVelocity: The release velocity, 0 to 127 (64 when in doubt).

Example:
midiNoteOff tOut, 1, 60, 64
*/
public handler midiNoteOff(in pHandle as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pNote as Integer, in pVelocity as Integer)
   send3(pHandle, 128 + chanNib(pChannel), pNote, pVelocity, 3)
end handler

/**
Summary: Sends a Control Change.

pHandle: The handle of an open output.

pChannel: The channel, 1 to 16.

pController: The controller number, 0 to 127 (7 is volume, 1 the mod wheel).

pValue: The value, 0 to 127.

Example:
midiControlChange tOut, 1, 7, 96
*/
public handler midiControlChange(in pHandle as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pController as Integer, in pValue as Integer)
   send3(pHandle, 176 + chanNib(pChannel), pController, pValue, 3)
end handler

/**
Summary: Sends a Program Change.

pHandle: The handle of an open output.

pChannel: The channel, 1 to 16.

pProgram: The program number as it travels, 0 to 127 (many instruments show
it as 1 to 128).

Example:
midiProgramChange tOut, 1, 4
*/
public handler midiProgramChange(in pHandle as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pProgram as Integer)
   send3(pHandle, 192 + chanNib(pChannel), pProgram, 0, 2)
end handler

/**
Summary: Sends Channel Pressure (mono aftertouch).

pHandle: The handle of an open output.

pChannel: The channel, 1 to 16.

pValue: The pressure, 0 to 127.

Example:
midiChannelPressure tOut, 1, 80
*/
public handler midiChannelPressure(in pHandle as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pValue as Integer)
   send3(pHandle, 208 + chanNib(pChannel), pValue, 0, 2)
end handler

/**
Summary: Sends a Pitch Bend.

pHandle: The handle of an open output.

pChannel: The channel, 1 to 16.

pValue14bit: The bend, 0 to 16383; 8192 is no bend.

Example:
midiPitchBend tOut, 1, 8192

Description: A value outside 0 to 16383 is clamped into it.
*/
public handler midiPitchBend(in pHandle as Integer, in pChannel as Integer, in pValue14bit as Integer)
   -- Clamp to the 14-bit range first; otherwise a negative or >16383 value makes
   -- the mod / intDiv below produce out-of-range (or negative) data bytes.
   variable tVal as Integer
   variable tLsb as Integer
   variable tMsb as Integer
   put pValue14bit into tVal
   if tVal < 0 then
      put 0 into tVal
   end if
   if tVal > 16383 then
      put 16383 into tVal
   end if
   put tVal mod 128 into tLsb
   put intDiv(tVal, 128) mod 128 into tMsb
   send3(pHandle, 224 + chanNib(pChannel), tLsb, tMsb, 3)
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Receiving
-- =====================================================================
-- midiPoll(pHandle) -> List of records, each:
--   ["delta": <seconds>, "bytes": <Data>, "kind": "noteOn",
--    "channel": 1, "data1": 60, "data2": 100]
/**
Summary: Collects the messages an input has received since the last call.

pHandle: The handle <midiOpenInput> or <midiOpenVirtualInput> returned.

Returns: A list of events, oldest first (empty when nothing is waiting). Each
event is an array: "kind" (such as "noteOn" or "controlChange"), "channel"
(1 to 16), "data1", "data2", "value14" for a pitch bend, "bytes" (the raw
message) and "delta" (seconds since the message before it).

Example:
repeat for each element tEvent in midiPoll(tIn)
   if tEvent["kind"] is "noteOn" then playNote tEvent["data1"], tEvent["data2"]
end repeat

Description: Call it on a timer: messages wait in a queue between calls, with
their timing, so a slow timer adds delay but loses nothing. One call returns
at most 256 events; call again at once when you get 256. A handle that is not
an open input (closed, or an output) returns an empty list, and
<midiLastError> says so.
*/
public handler midiPoll(in pHandle as Integer) returns List
   variable tBytes as Integer
   variable tDrain as Data
   variable tOk as Boolean
   variable tEvents as List
   variable tPos as Integer
   variable tLen as Integer
   variable tMsg as Data
   variable tDeltaUs as Integer
   put [] into tEvents
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   ensureDrainBuf()
   unsafe
      put _in_drain_bytes(pHandle, sDrainPtr, kDrainCap, 256) into tBytes
   end unsafe
   if tBytes <= 0 then
      return tEvents
   end if
   -- Copy exactly the drained bytes out of the raw block (ONE copy per poll, of
   -- only what was written -- not the whole 64 KB block).
   unsafe
      put MCDataCreateWithBytes(sDrainPtr, tBytes, tDrain) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      return tEvents
   end if
   -- Walk the self-delimiting records, never past the bytes the shim wrote:
   -- [2 bytes len BE][len bytes message][4 bytes delta micros BE]
   put 1 into tPos          -- 1-based byte index into tDrain
   repeat while tPos + 5 <= tBytes
      put the code of (byte tPos of tDrain) * 256 + the code of (byte (tPos + 1) of tDrain) into tLen
      put tPos + 2 into tPos
      if tPos + tLen + 3 > tBytes then
         exit repeat        -- truncated record: cannot happen with a well-behaved shim
      end if
      if tLen > 0 then
         put byte tPos to (tPos + tLen - 1) of tDrain into tMsg
      else
         put the empty data into tMsg
      end if
      put tPos + tLen into tPos
      put the code of (byte tPos of tDrain) * 16777216 \
        + the code of (byte (tPos + 1) of tDrain) * 65536 \
        + the code of (byte (tPos + 2) of tDrain) * 256 \
        + the code of (byte (tPos + 3) of tDrain) into tDeltaUs
      put tPos + 4 into tPos
      push decodeEvent(tMsg, tDeltaUs) onto tEvents
   end repeat
   return tEvents
end handler

-- Decode a raw MIDI message + delta into a semantic record.
private handler decodeEvent(in pBytes as Data, in pDeltaUs as Integer) returns Array
   variable tRec as Array
   variable tN as Integer
   variable tStatus as Integer
   variable tD1 as Integer
   variable tD2 as Integer
   variable tType as Integer
   put (pDeltaUs / 1000000) into tRec["delta"]
   put pBytes into tRec["bytes"]
   put the number of bytes in pBytes into tN
   if tN is 0 then
      put "empty" into tRec["kind"]
      return tRec
   end if
   put the code of (byte 1 of pBytes) into tStatus
   put 0 into tD1
   put 0 into tD2
   if tN >= 2 then
      put the code of (byte 2 of pBytes) into tD1
   end if
   if tN >= 3 then
      put the code of (byte 3 of pBytes) into tD2
   end if
   put tD1 into tRec["data1"]
   put tD2 into tRec["data2"]
   if tStatus >= 240 then
      -- system messages (0xF0..0xFF); SysEx etc.
      put 0 into tRec["channel"]
      if tStatus is 240 then
         put "sysex" into tRec["kind"]
      else
         put "system" into tRec["kind"]
      end if
      return tRec
   end if
   put tStatus - (tStatus mod 16) into tType    -- high nibble
   put (tStatus mod 16) + 1 into tRec["channel"]
   if tType is 144 then
      if tD2 is 0 then
         put "noteOff" into tRec["kind"]      -- note-on velocity 0 == note-off
      else
         put "noteOn" into tRec["kind"]
      end if
   else if tType is 128 then
      put "noteOff" into tRec["kind"]
   else if tType is 176 then
      put "controlChange" into tRec["kind"]
   else if tType is 192 then
      put "programChange" into tRec["kind"]
   else if tType is 224 then
      put "pitchBend" into tRec["kind"]
      put tD1 + tD2 * 128 into tRec["value14"]
   else if tType is 208 then
      put "channelPressure" into tRec["kind"]
   else if tType is 160 then
      put "polyAftertouch" into tRec["kind"]
   else
      put "other" into tRec["kind"]
   end if
   return tRec
end handler

-- midiDecode(pBytes) -> Array
--   Decode one raw MIDI message (Data) into the SAME semantic record midiPoll
--   produces -- ["kind", "channel", "data1", "data2", "value14", "bytes", "delta"]
--   -- with delta 0. Useful for decoding MIDI that arrives from somewhere other
--   than a polled input (a file, a Web-MIDI bridge, a test harness), and it makes
--   the decode logic verifiable with NO MIDI hardware at all.
/**
Summary: Reads one MIDI message into the same event array <midiPoll> returns.

pBytes: One raw MIDI message, as binary data.

Returns: An array with "kind", "channel", "data1", "data2", "value14" (pitch
bend only), "bytes" and "delta" (0).

Example:
put midiDecode(numToByte(144) & numToByte(60) & numToByte(100)) into tEvent
-- tEvent["kind"] is "noteOn", tEvent["channel"] is 1

Description: For MIDI that comes from somewhere other than an input, such as
a file, and for testing a stack's MIDI handling with no MIDI device at all.
*/
public handler midiDecode(in pBytes as Data) returns Array
   return decodeEvent(pBytes, 0)
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Errors, and loading the library by hand
-- =====================================================================
/**
Summary: Says why the last MIDI call failed.

Returns: The reason, or empty when the last call that lists, opens, sends to
or polls a port succeeded.

Example:
put midiOpenOutput(99) into tOut
if tOut is 0 then answer midiLastError()

Description: Read it straight after the call that failed: the next such call
replaces it.
*/
public handler midiLastError() returns String
   if sBindingError is not the empty string then
      return sBindingError
   end if
   return shimText(kTxtError, 0)
end handler

-- Linux helper: preload midixt.so by absolute path (no /usr/lib, no sudo, no
-- LD_LIBRARY_PATH). Returns 1 on success, 0 on failure (then midiLoadNativeLibError).
-- Flags 258 = RTLD_NOW | RTLD_GLOBAL. Gate the call to Linux when wiring it in.
/**
Summary: Loads the native library from a path you choose (Linux).

pPath: The full path of midixt.so.

Returns: 1 when it loaded, 0 when it did not (<midiLoadNativeLibError> says
why).

Example:
if the platform is "Linux" then get midiLoadNativeLib(tFolder & "/midixt.so")

Description: Only needed when the library is not where the engine looks for
it. Call it on Linux only, before any other call of this library.
*/
public handler midiLoadNativeLib(in pPath as String) returns Integer
   variable tHandle as optional Pointer
   put the empty string into sLoadError
   if hasNul(pPath) then
      put "midixt: the path contains a NUL character" into sLoadError
      return 0
   end if
   bindLoaderErrorPath()
   unsafe
      put _dl_open(pPath, 258) into tHandle
   end unsafe
   if tHandle is nothing then
      put loaderErrorNow() into sLoadError
      return 0
   end if
   return 1
end handler

/**
Summary: Says why <midiLoadNativeLib> could not load the library.

Returns: The system's reason for the last <midiLoadNativeLib> that returned
0, or empty when the last one loaded the library (or none has run).

Description: The reason is read when the load fails and kept until the next
<midiLoadNativeLib>, so it can be asked for at any time after.
*/
public handler midiLoadNativeLibError() returns String
   return sLoadError
end handler

-- The engine binds a foreign handler on its FIRST call, through dlsym, and
-- that call consumes or frees the error text of a dlopen that just failed
-- (engine note 6.19). So the two handlers the error path needs are called
-- once BEFORE the dlopen whose error they read: the string copy (on a
-- one-byte empty C string) and dlerror itself, which also clears an older
-- error first, the POSIX idiom.
private handler bindLoaderErrorPath()
   variable tNulByte as Data
   variable tNulPtr as Pointer
   variable tEmpty as String
   variable tOk as Boolean
   variable tOlder as optional Pointer
   put the byte with code 0 into tNulByte
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetBytePtr(tNulByte) into tNulPtr
      put MCStringCreateWithCString(tNulPtr, tEmpty) into tOk
      put _dl_error() into tOlder
   end unsafe
end handler

-- dlerror()'s text, COPIED at once: it is libc's, so it comes back as a raw
-- Pointer and never as a ZString the engine would free (engine note 6.18).
private handler loaderErrorNow() returns String
   variable tPtr as optional Pointer
   variable tErr as String
   variable tOk as Boolean
   unsafe
      put _dl_error() into tPtr
   end unsafe
   if tPtr is nothing then
      return "midixt: the library did not load, and the loader gave no reason"
   end if
   unsafe
      put MCStringCreateWithCString(tPtr, tErr) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      return "midixt: the library did not load (its reason could not be read)"
   end if
   return tErr
end handler

end library
